      <h1>When the rent is due</h1>
      <p>This page was made by people who know what the end of the month can feel like. It is peer support and general information — not legal advice, and not a crisis service. If the money stress turns into something darker, stop and use the <a href="/resources/tonight/">For tonight</a> page, or call or text <a href="tel:988">988</a>.</p>
      <h2>Start with one call: 2-1-1</h2>
      <p>Dial <a href="tel:211">2-1-1</a> from any phone. It's free, confidential, and staffed by people whose whole job is knowing what help exists near you — rent assistance, utility help, food, and more, in over 200 languages. In San Joaquin County, <a href="https://211sj.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">211 San Joaquin</a> can also be reached at <a href="tel:+18004369997">1-800-436-9997</a>, or by texting your ZIP code to <a href="sms:898211">898211</a>.</p>
      <h2>Talk to your landlord before the due date, not after</h2>
      <p>Silence reads as not caring. A short conversation before the date passes reads as a person with a plan. Ask about a payment plan, a split payment, or a short extension — and get whatever you agree on in writing, even if it's just a text you save.</p>
      <p>A script, if you need one: "I'm writing because my rent will be short this month. I can pay part on the due date and the rest two weeks later. I wanted you to hear it from me before the due date, not after." You don't owe anyone your whole story. The dates and the amounts are enough.</p>
      <h2>Know the shape of it</h2>
      <p>A pay-or-quit notice is not an eviction. In California, only a court can order you to leave, and that process takes time — time you can use. Don't move out in a panic over a notice alone. Free HUD-approved housing counselors can talk through your specific situation: <a href="tel:+18005694287">1-800-569-4287</a>.</p>
      <h2>Utilities and food: take the pressure off where you can</h2>
      <p>Call your utility before a shutoff, not after — most offer payment plans, and discount and assistance programs exist for exactly this. Ask 211 what you qualify for. For food, <a href="https://www.localfoodbank.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Second Harvest of the Greater Valley</a> (<a href="tel:+12092392091">209-239-2091</a>) serves San Joaquin and Stanislaus counties. Using the food bank for a month is not failure — it's how you keep the rent money pointed at the rent.</p>
      <h2>Triage, in order</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Roof</strong> — the rent, or the payment plan you agreed to.</li>
        <li><strong>Utilities</strong> — the minimum that keeps the lights and water on.</li>
        <li><strong>Food</strong> — with the food bank carrying part of it.</li>
        <li><strong>Everything else</strong> — minimum payments, and a call to any lender you can't pay, before the due date. Same as the landlord.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>One call a day is progress. You don't have to fix the whole spiral tonight.</p>
